WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.083 --> 00:00:10.766 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to episode four of our U.S. states series. First, I clear the map so
we can study a fresh group.

2
00:00:11.417 --> 00:00:25.361 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Today we explore all twelve Midwest states, from the Great Lakes through the
Mississippi Valley to the central Plains. Lakes, river borders and north-south
pairs will guide us.

3
00:00:26.019 --> 00:00:33.008 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's begin with Ohio on Lake Erie, then travel around the Great Lakes and
west across the Midwest.

4
00:00:35.792 --> 00:00:47.901 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ohio sits immediately west of Pennsylvania, above Kentucky. Its northern edge
reaches Lake Erie; the Ohio River curves along much of its southern border.

5
00:00:48.551 --> 00:00:57.978 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name comes from a Seneca expression for a good or great river. The Wright
brothers developed their aircraft in Dayton, before flying in North Carolina.

6
00:00:58.630 --> 00:01:07.837 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Use Ohio as our eastern gateway to the Midwest. Indiana lies to its west,
while Michigan reaches down from the north.

7
00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:16.920 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We'll widen the view northward for Michigan. Its two main peninsulas need to
be seen together, across the Great Lakes.

8
00:01:19.241 --> 00:01:30.456 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Michigan has a mitten-shaped Lower Peninsula and a separate Upper Peninsula
extending west above Lake Michigan. Both belong to the same state.

9
00:01:31.122 --> 00:01:41.617 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name refers to large water. The Mackinac Bridge connects the two
peninsulas across the Straits of Mackinac; it opened in 1957.

10
00:01:42.269 --> 00:01:52.892 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Do not mistake the Upper Peninsula for Wisconsin. Michigan's two parts frame
the northern end of Lake Michigan, while Indiana is farther south.

11
00:01:53.587 --> 00:02:02.921 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Now follow Lake Michigan south. We'll study Indiana and Illinois side by side
before continuing up the lake's western shore.

12
00:02:05.241 --> 00:02:13.507 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Indiana lies west of Ohio and east of Illinois, with Kentucky below it. Its
short northern shoreline touches Lake Michigan.

13
00:02:14.171 --> 00:02:24.109 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name means land of the Indians, a name given by settlers. The first
Indianapolis 500 motor race was held here in 1911.

14
00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:35.939 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Indiana as the middle state in this east-west sequence: Ohio,
Indiana, Illinois. We move one state west next.

15
00:02:38.257 --> 00:02:49.646 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Illinois is the long state west of Indiana. Chicago sits in its northeastern
corner on Lake Michigan, while the Mississippi River forms its western edge.

16
00:02:50.297 --> 00:03:03.602 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes through French from the Illinois peoples, and the final s is
silent. Chicago rebuilt after its 1871 fire and became a centre of early
skyscraper design.

17
00:03:04.258 --> 00:03:13.302 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Keep Illinois between Indiana and the Mississippi. Wisconsin lies directly
above it, along the western side of Lake Michigan.

18
00:03:13.998 --> 00:03:22.787 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's move north and west to Wisconsin and Minnesota. The Great Lakes give way
to the upper Mississippi and a landscape of many smaller lakes.

19
00:03:25.121 --> 00:03:35.442 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Wisconsin lies north of Illinois, between Minnesota and Lake Michigan. Lake
Superior touches its northern edge, below Michigan's Upper Peninsula.

20
00:03:36.101 --> 00:03:47.955 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The card highlights Wisconsin's cheese-making identity. Dairy farming and
cheese production are central to the state's America’s Dairyland nickname.

21
00:03:48.613 --> 00:03:59.526 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Wisconsin on Lake Michigan's western shore. Minnesota is immediately
to the west, across river borders in the south and on land farther north.

22
00:04:02.063 --> 00:04:11.908 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Minnesota is west of Wisconsin and north of Iowa. Its northeastern tip reaches
Lake Superior, and its northern boundary meets Canada.

23
00:04:12.565 --> 00:04:24.547 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its Dakota-derived name is associated with sky-tinted or cloudy water. The
Mississippi River begins here at Lake Itasca, far from its mouth in Louisiana.

24
00:04:25.205 --> 00:04:35.608 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Keep Minnesota above Iowa and east of the Dakotas. Our next move follows the
Mississippi south, toward Iowa and Missouri.

25
00:04:36.294 --> 00:04:45.036 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We now move south along the Mississippi Valley. Iowa and Missouri form another
useful north-south pair.

26
00:04:47.350 --> 00:04:56.603 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Iowa lies directly below Minnesota, with the Mississippi along its eastern
edge. Nebraska is to its west and Missouri below it.

27
00:04:57.268 --> 00:05:07.891 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The name comes from the Ioway people. The card points to Iowa's farming
identity; corn production is a major part of the state's agriculture.

28
00:05:08.547 --> 00:05:16.813 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Iowa between the Missouri River on the west and the Mississippi on
the east. Missouri, our next state, is directly south.

29
00:05:19.162 --> 00:05:29.739 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Missouri sits below Iowa and west of Illinois. The Mississippi forms its
eastern boundary; the Missouri River crosses the state toward St Louis.

30
00:05:30.389 --> 00:05:40.107 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The card connects Missouri with westward travel. In St Louis, the Gateway Arch
commemorates the city's role in the westward expansion of the United States.

31
00:05:40.758 --> 00:05:50.058 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Look for Missouri's small southeastern bootheel. Kansas lies to the west; we
now turn toward the central Plains.

32
00:05:50.825 --> 00:05:59.091 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's frame Kansas and Nebraska, two broad Plains states stacked north and
south. Then we'll continue north through the Dakotas.

33
00:06:02.809 --> 00:06:14.454 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kansas lies west of Missouri, directly above Oklahoma and below Nebraska. Most
of its outline is rectangular, apart from its northeastern river boundary.

34
00:06:15.114 --> 00:06:29.406 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from the Kansa people. The Topeka case in Brown versus Board of
Education helped lead to the 1954 ruling that racial segregation in public
schools was unconstitutional.

35
00:06:30.069 --> 00:06:40.437 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Kansas above Oklahoma and below Nebraska. Unlike Arkansas, its final
s is pronounced; those are two different states.

36
00:06:43.386 --> 00:06:53.881 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nebraska stretches across the Plains above Kansas. Notice the notch in its
southwestern corner and the Missouri River along its eastern side.

37
00:06:54.534 --> 00:07:06.992 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name refers to flat water, linked to the Platte River. Nebraska has the
country's only one-house state legislature, officially organised on a
nonpartisan basis.

38
00:07:07.644 --> 00:07:16.386 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Think of Nebraska as the next rung north from Kansas. South Dakota lies above
it; Iowa is across the Missouri River to the east.

39
00:07:17.089 --> 00:07:26.516 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Our final close view takes in both Dakotas, from Nebraska's northern border up
to Canada. Watch the two stacked outlines.

40
00:07:28.834 --> 00:07:39.028 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
South Dakota lies above Nebraska and below North Dakota. Minnesota is to the
east, while the Black Hills are in the western part of the state.

41
00:07:39.685 --> 00:07:54.360 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The card recalls Mount Rushmore, carved between 1927 and 1941 in the Black
Hills. The area is sacred to the Lakota, and its history remains contested.

42
00:07:55.019 --> 00:08:03.285 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember South Dakota as the lower Dakota. Its northern neighbour shares both
its name and the day it became a state.

43
00:08:05.619 --> 00:08:15.940 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
North Dakota is directly above South Dakota, along the Canadian border.
Minnesota is to its east and Montana to its west.

44
00:08:16.602 --> 00:08:30.255 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Dakota means friend or ally. Both Dakotas joined the Union on November second,
1889; the signing papers were shuffled so the order was not recorded.

45
00:08:30.913 --> 00:08:45.588 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
North Dakota completes our twelve Midwest states. Trace the Plains stack from
south to north: Kansas, Nebraska, South Dakota, North Dakota.

46
00:08:50.585 --> 00:09:00.175 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Start with Ohio, Michigan, Indiana and Illinois; continue around the lakes to
Wisconsin and Minnesota. Then locate Iowa above Missouri.

47
00:09:00.826 --> 00:09:12.424 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Finish with the Plains stack: Kansas, Nebraska, South Dakota and North Dakota.
That completes all twelve Midwest states. One final episode will take us
across the West.
